    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d693">

                <pb facs="0805" n="717" sameAs="frus:frus1904/pg717"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Hay to Mr.
                            McCormick.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1904-04-11"><hi rendition="#italic">April 11,
                        1904</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 96.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I inclose copy of a note of the 9th instant
                    from the Japanese minister at this capital,<anchor n="a" xml:id="d693-f1"/>
                    expressing the hope of his Government that this Government will use its good
                    offices with Russia to obtain the release of the Japanese on board the press
                    boat <hi rendition="#italic">Hanyei Maru</hi>, chartered by a correspondent of
                    the Asahi Shinbun.</p>
                <p>The men in question are said to have been noncombatants and not engaged in the
                    commission of any act hostile to Russia.</p>
                <p>The minister points out that his Government has released all of the passengers on
                    board of the Russian merchant vessels captured, also their officers and crew,
                    except those whose evidence was needed in the admiralty courts.</p>
                <p>You will endeavor to carry out the desire of the Japanese Government.</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">John Hay.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
